.TH GETTOKEN 1 "2026-09-06" "gettoken" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gettoken \- obtain a narrow access token for one job
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gettoken
.I capability
.br
.B gettoken
.B \-\-list
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gettoken
is the entry point an agent uses to obtain an access token. It holds no
credential of its own. It forwards what it was asked for to the privileged half
of the host, which draws on a super\-token a human already placed there and
returns a token narrow enough for the named job and nothing more.
.PP
A capability is a name, and it is not a secret. Its first segment names the
service, and the segments after it name what is wanted of that service, as in
.IR github/thruput\-io/gettoken/pr/create .
.PP
A successful run writes the access token, and nothing else, to standard output.
A failure writes to standard error and leaves standard output empty, so a
caller cannot mistake a message for a credential.
.PP
The token is not cached and its lifetime is not reported. Ask again for each
job that needs one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-list
Write the capabilities this agent may equip, one per line. A capability that is
not listed is refused.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A token was issued and written to standard output.
.TP
.B 1
No token was issued. The reason is on standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/gettoken
The privileged half:
.BR token\-requester ,
.BR token\-service ,
.B entitlements
and the programs that reach the store.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/gettoken/exchangers
One exchanger per service, named for the first segment of the capability it
serves. Installed by the package that integrates that service.
.TP
.I /var/lib/gettoken/secrets
The super-tokens a human placed on the privileged side. No account but that one
can read it, and nothing an agent runs reaches it.
.SH SEE ALSO
The design and the vocabulary are described in the README under
.IR /usr/share/doc/gettoken .
